# The stories were written for this lesson.
lesson reading-what-happens-next v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=8 subject=reading levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What happens next"
  goal "Predict what will happen next in a story, and point to the detail that makes it likely."

  look {
    scene 34x16 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        passage p text="Zara filled the beaker right to the top. She picked it up and hurried across the lab. Her foot caught on a bag strap." width=20 numbers=false
        beaker b max=500 step=100 level=500 unit=ml
      }
    }
    say """
      Good readers guess what will happen next, and their guesses are not wild. They use the
      details: a beaker filled to the top, a hurry, a foot caught on a strap. Some details are
      said, and some you work out. A prediction can change: if a new sentence changes the facts,
      change your guess too.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked reading.predict-next k=0
    }
    show reading.predict-next k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show reading.predict-clue k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show reading.predict-next k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show reading.predict-character k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  story {
    show reading.predict-ruled-out k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show reading.predict-clue k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
    level medium hard {
      show reading.predict-next k=4 hard-k=3
    }
    show reading.predict-character k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
    show reading.predict-ruled-out k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show reading.predict-change k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show reading.predict-write k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "A good prediction comes from the details. Say which detail, and change it when the facts change."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a prediction is reasoned from details the text states or implies, so the child
    always says which detail. The way in predicts what comes next after a beaker filled to the top;
    the core finds the one sentence that leads to a prediction (past a detail that only looks like
    a clue), predicts from what a character is always like, and finds the prediction a detail rules
    out. The wrong idea to watch for is the prediction that could happen but has nothing in the
    story behind it, and, in the two-star, keeping an old prediction after the facts change. If the
    child is stuck, read the story aloud and stop before the last sentence each time, asking what
    they expect and why. Off the page, pause a bedtime story before turning a page and ask for a
    prediction and its clue, then read on to check. England's programme of study for years 3 and 4
    asks for "predicting what might happen from details stated and implied".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the beaker is worked in the teacher's pen first, the clues are said outright (ice in the sun, a plant in the dark), the clue sentence is the trainers on wet grass, the ruled-out prediction is stopped by the lid, and the two-star is the bean plant."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the clues are implied (a door open a crack, a label that says no water), the clue sentence sits past the jelly's warm water, the ruled-out prediction turns on a price, the character is Gran and her jars, and the three-star weighs two next events."
  }
}
